analysis:
In `public/app/plugins/panel/state-timeline/migrations.ts`, `timelinePanelChangedHandler` handles migrating panel options from legacy panels (`natel-discrete-panel`).

When processing `oldOptions.valueMaps`, the code iterates over user-controlled `valueMaps` entries:
```typescript
const text: string = p.text;
const value: string = p.value;
if (text && value) {
  let old = valuemap.options[value];
  if (old) {
    old.text = text;
  } else {
    valuemap.options[value] = { text };
  }
}
```
Because `valuemap.options` is initialized as an ordinary JavaScript object (`{}`), it inherits from `Object.prototype`. If an untrusted dashboard configuration contains an entry where `value` is `'__proto__'`, `valuemap.options[value]` resolves to `Object.prototype`. As `old` is truthy (`old === Object.prototype`), the branch `old.text = text` is executed, which directly writes `Object.prototype.text = text`. This pollutes `Object.prototype` across the entire application runtime.

Conceptual Exploit Payload:
In a dashboard panel JSON (or during panel migration/switch from `natel-discrete-panel`):
```json
{
  "type": "natel-discrete-panel",
  "valueMaps": [
    {
      "value": "__proto__",
      "text": "polluted"
    }
  ]
}
```
When this panel is converted to a `state-timeline` panel, `timelinePanelChangedHandler` will execute `valuemap.options['__proto__'].text = 'polluted'`, resulting in `({}).text === 'polluted'`, leading to prototype pollution that can be leveraged for client-side XSS or application logic bypass.

